From 86a872610e41fd1634e529b1e3e74decd0c6bf2f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cezanne <port@local>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 04:40:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] panvk: v9 clip and cull distances through a fragment
 discard

The v9 rasterizer has no clip / cull distances, DXVK requires both. The
vertex shader writes them as ordinary varyings (they are not hardware
varying slots, so they are generic ones), the fragment shader
interpolates them and discards where an enabled one is negative. Which
ones the vertex shader writes is a draw-time sysval (clip bits 0-7,
cull 8-15), so separately compiled fragment shaders (pipeline
libraries, shader objects) work too; with a linked vertex shader only
its slots are read and nothing is added when it writes none.

Cull distances are approximated per fragment: a primitive whose
vertices are all negative is gone, a partly negative one is cut where
the spec keeps it.

The discard must not cost early ZS / forward pixel kill when no
distances are written: such shaders also keep the early ZS table and
FPK / discard flags without it, and the draw picks them when the bound
vertex shader writes none.
